              "failure": {
                "kind": "verification_failed",
                "failure_class": "bad_output",
                "evidence": "review changes_requested: The public Collection command polls every authorized account in duetflow/app.py:161-176, although duetflow/auth.py:24-29 and docs/PLAN.md section 5 grant user-read-recently-played only to role B. With the intended two-account setup, role A's recently-played request returns 403, cmd_collect marks the run failed, and `duetflow collect` exits 1 every time; if role A instead raises InvalidGrant, the ordered loop aborts before role B is polled and loses the listening evidence this phase exists to preserve.; The 401 recovery callback in duetflow/app.py:169-172 discards refresh-token rotation: when refresh_access_token returns TokenGrant(access='new-access', refresh='new-refresh', ...), it persists only the access token and expiry, leaving the old refresh token in accounts. The next access refresh can therefore present an obsolete credential and stop Collection or force premature re-consent; this is especially unsafe in the new risky Spotify auth/retry path.; The risky-file retry policy in duetflow/spotify.py:201-208 ignores Retry-After for QUOTA_EXCEEDED responses. A 429 carrying Retry-After: 1 and body {\"error\":{\"reason\":\"QUOTA_EXCEEDED\"}} makes one request, sleeps zero seconds, and fails immediately, even though docs/PLAN.md section 5 requires Retry-After to be honored for 429 values within the configured bound while distinguishing quota exhaustion from an ordinary rate limit. tests/test_collector.py:104-109 currently codifies this contrary behavior as fail-fast.",
                "retry_after_sec": null,
                "reset_at": null
              },
              "stepped_down": null,
              "impl_log": "/home/dan/projects/duetflow/.orchestrator/attempts/att_run_LisIoqe6p1xbHQj2_proof_review_02/impl.log",
              "transcript": null,
              "review": {
                "verdict": "changes_requested",
                "findings": [
                  "The public Collection command polls every authorized account in duetflow/app.py:161-176, although duetflow/auth.py:24-29 and docs/PLAN.md section 5 grant user-read-recently-played only to role B. With the intended two-account setup, role A's recently-played request returns 403, cmd_collect marks the run failed, and `duetflow collect` exits 1 every time; if role A instead raises InvalidGrant, the ordered loop aborts before role B is polled and loses the listening evidence this phase exists to preserve.",
                  "The 401 recovery callback in duetflow/app.py:169-172 discards refresh-token rotation: when refresh_access_token returns TokenGrant(access='new-access', refresh='new-refresh', ...), it persists only the access token and expiry, leaving the old refresh token in accounts. The next access refresh can therefore present an obsolete credential and stop Collection or force premature re-consent; this is especially unsafe in the new risky Spotify auth/retry path.",
                  "The risky-file retry policy in duetflow/spotify.py:201-208 ignores Retry-After for QUOTA_EXCEEDED responses. A 429 carrying Retry-After: 1 and body {\"error\":{\"reason\":\"QUOTA_EXCEEDED\"}} makes one request, sleeps zero seconds, and fails immediately, even though docs/PLAN.md section 5 requires Retry-After to be honored for 429 values within the configured bound while distinguishing quota exhaustion from an ordinary rate limit. tests/test_collector.py:104-109 currently codifies this contrary behavior as fail-fast."
                ],
